.SH "NAME"
octa \- multi\-format tabular data viewer, editor, CLI tool, and MCP server
.SH "SYNOPSIS"
.sp
\fBocta\fP [\fIFILE\fP.\|.\|.]
.sp
\fBocta\fP \fB\-\-schema\fP \fIFILE\fP [\fB\-f\fP \fIFORMAT\fP] [\fB\-\-rows\fP \fIN\fP|all]
.sp
\fBocta\fP \fB\-\-head\fP \fIFILE\fP [\fB\-n\fP \fIN\fP] [\fB\-f\fP \fIFORMAT\fP] [\fB\-\-rows\fP \fIN\fP|all]
.sp
\fBocta\fP \fB\-\-tail\fP \fIFILE\fP [\fB\-n\fP \fIN\fP] [\fB\-f\fP \fIFORMAT\fP] [\fB\-\-rows\fP \fIN\fP|all]
.sp
\fBocta\fP \fB\-\-sample\fP \fIFILE\fP [\fB\-n\fP \fIN\fP] [\fB\-\-seed\fP \fIN\fP] [\fB\-f\fP \fIFORMAT\fP] [\fB\-\-rows\fP \fIN\fP|all]
.sp
\fBocta\fP \fB\-\-convert\fP \fIIN\fP \fIOUT\fP [\fB\-\-rows\fP \fIN\fP|all]
.sp
\fBocta\fP \fB\-\-sql\fP \fIFILE\fP \fB\-q\fP \fIQUERY\fP [\fB\-f\fP \fIFORMAT\fP] [\fB\-\-rows\fP \fIN\fP|all] [\fB\-\-sql\-table\fP \fINAME\fP=\fIPATH\fP .\|.\|.] [\fB\-\-sql\-attach\fP \fIALIAS\fP=\fIPATH\fP .\|.\|.] [\fB\-\-sql\-write\-to\fP \fIPATH\fP \fB\-\-sql\-write\-table\fP \fITABLE\fP [\fB\-\-sql\-write\-schema\fP \fISCHEMA\fP] [\fB\-\-sql\-write\-mode\fP \fIMODE\fP]]
.sp
\fBocta\fP \fB\-\-export\-schema\fP \fIFILE\fP [\fB\-t\fP \fITARGET\fP]
.sp
\fBocta\fP \fB\-\-compare\-schemas\fP \fIFILE_A\fP \fIFILE_B\fP [\fB\-\-table\-a\fP \fINAME\fP] [\fB\-\-table\-b\fP \fINAME\fP] [\fB\-f\fP \fIFORMAT\fP]
.sp
\fBocta\fP \fB\-\-diff\fP \fIFILE_A\fP \fIFILE_B\fP [\fB\-\-diff\-mode\fP \fIMODE\fP] [\fB\-\-diff\-on\fP \fICOLS\fP] [\fB\-f\fP \fIFORMAT\fP]
.sp
\fBocta\fP \fB\-\-describe\fP \fIFILE\fP [\fB\-\-table\fP \fINAME\fP] [\fB\-\-sample\-rows\fP \fIN\fP] [\fB\-f\fP \fIFORMAT\fP]
.sp
\fBocta\fP \fB\-\-validate\-schema\fP \fIFILE\fP \fB\-\-expect\-schema\fP \fISCHEMA_FILE\fP [\fB\-\-table\fP \fINAME\fP] [\fB\-f\fP \fIFORMAT\fP]
.sp
\fBocta\fP \fB\-\-schema\-drift\fP \fIDIR\fP [\fB\-\-recursive\fP] [\fB\-\-ignore\-case\fP] [\fB\-f\fP \fIFORMAT\fP]
.sp
\fBocta\fP \fB\-\-relationships\fP \fIDIR\fP [\fB\-\-recursive\fP] [\fB\-f\fP \fIFORMAT\fP]
.sp
\fBocta\fP \fB\-\-check\fP \fIFILE\fP \fB\-\-rules\fP \fIRULES.toml\fP [\fB\-f\fP \fIFORMAT\fP]
.sp
\fBocta\fP \fB\-\-drift\-report\fP \fIFILE_A\fP \fIFILE_B\fP [\fB\-\-fail\-on\fP \fISPEC\fP] [\fB\-f\fP \fIFORMAT\fP]
.sp
\fBocta\fP \fB\-\-harmonise\-schema\fP \fIDIR\fP \fB\-\-out\-dir\fP \fIDIR\fP [\fB\-\-target\-file\fP \fIFILE\fP]
[\fB\-\-recursive\fP] [\fB\-\-ignore\-case\fP] [\fB\-\-overwrite\fP]
.sp
\fBocta\fP \fB\-\-report\fP \fIOUT.html\fP \fIFILE\fP [\fB\-\-report\-sample\fP \fIN\fP] [\fB\-\-report\-sections\fP \fILIST\fP]
.sp
\fBocta\fP \fB\-\-unique\-columns\fP \fIFILE\fP [\fB\-\-table\fP \fINAME\fP] [\fB\-\-max\-combo\fP \fIN\fP] [\fB\-f\fP \fIFORMAT\fP]
.sp
\fBocta\fP \fB\-\-anonymize\fP \fISPEC\fP \fIFILE\fP [\fB\-f\fP \fIFORMAT\fP]
.sp
\fBocta\fP \fB\-\-dedupe\fP \fIFILE\fP [\fB\-\-dedupe\-on\fP \fICOLS\fP] [\fB\-\-dedupe\-keep\fP \fIWHICH\fP] [\fB\-f\fP \fIFORMAT\fP]
.sp
\fBocta\fP \fB\-\-impute\fP \fICOL\fP=\fISTRATEGY\fP \fIFILE\fP [\fB\-f\fP \fIFORMAT\fP]
.sp
\fBocta\fP \fB\-\-outliers\fP \fIFILE\fP [\fB\-\-outlier\-method\fP \fIM\fP] [\fB\-\-outlier\-cols\fP \fICOLS\fP] [\fB\-\-outlier\-k\fP \fIK\fP] [\fB\-f\fP \fIFORMAT\fP]
.sp
\fBocta\fP \fB\-\-detect\-pii\fP \fIFILE\fP [\fB\-\-pii\-sample\fP \fIN\fP] [\fB\-f\fP \fIFORMAT\fP]
.sp
\fBocta\fP \fB\-\-union\fP \fIFILE\fP \fB\-\-union\-file\fP \fIFILE\fP [\fB\-\-union\-drop\fP \fICOL\fP] [\fB\-\-union\-cast\fP \fICOL\fP=\fITYPE\fP] [\fB\-\-union\-ignore\-case\fP] [\fB\-f\fP \fIFORMAT\fP]
.sp
\fBocta\fP \fB\-\-join\fP \fIFILE\fP \fB\-\-join\-file\fP \fIFILE\fP \fB\-\-join\-on\fP \fICOLS\fP [\fB\-\-join\-type\fP \fITYPE\fP] [\fB\-f\fP \fIFORMAT\fP]
.sp
\fBocta\fP \fB\-\-fuzzy\-join\fP \fIFILE\fP \fB\-\-fuzzy\-join\-file\fP \fIFILE\fP \fB\-\-fuzzy\-on\fP \fILEFT\fP=\fIRIGHT\fP [\fB\-\-fuzzy\-method\fP \fINAME\fP] [\fB\-\-fuzzy\-threshold\fP \fIN\fP] [\fB\-\-fuzzy\-block\fP \fILEFT\fP=\fIRIGHT\fP] [\fB\-\-fuzzy\-join\-type\fP \fITYPE\fP] [\fB\-\-fuzzy\-max\-rows\fP \fIN\fP] [\fB\-f\fP \fIFORMAT\fP]
.sp
\fBocta\fP \fB\-\-partition\-by\fP \fICOL\fP \fB\-\-out\-dir\fP \fIDIR\fP \fIFILE\fP [\fB\-\-partition\-format\fP \fIEXT\fP]
.sp
\fBocta\fP \fB\-\-db\-tables\fP \fB\-\-db\fP \fICONNECTION\fP [\fB\-\-db\-catalog\fP \fINAME\fP] [\fB\-f\fP \fIFORMAT\fP]
.sp
\fBocta\fP \fB\-\-db\-query\fP \fISQL\fP \fB\-\-db\fP \fICONNECTION\fP [\fB\-f\fP \fIFORMAT\fP]
.sp
\fBocta\fP \fB\-\-sync\-sql\fP \fIFILE\fP \fB\-\-db\fP \fICONNECTION\fP \fB\-\-sync\-table\fP \fISCHEMA.TABLE\fP \fB\-\-sync\-on\fP \fICOLS\fP
.sp
\fBocta\fP \fB\-\-to\-workbook\fP \fIOUT.xlsx\fP \fIFILE\fP.\|.\|.
.sp
\fBocta\fP \fB\-\-convert\fP \fIIN\fP \fB\-\fP \fB\-\-to\fP \fIEXT\fP
.sp
\fBocta\fP \fB\-\-db\-write\-table\fP \fISCHEMA\fP.\fITABLE\fP \fB\-\-db\fP \fICONNECTION\fP \fIFILE\fP [\fB\-\-db\-write\-mode\fP \fIMODE\fP] [\fB\-\-db\-catalog\fP \fINAME\fP]
.sp
\fBocta\fP \fB\-\-db\-copy\fP \fISCHEMA\fP.\fITABLE\fP \fB\-\-db\fP \fICONNECTION\fP \fB\-\-db\-copy\-to\fP \fICONNECTION\fP [\fB\-\-db\-copy\-target\fP \fISCHEMA\fP.\fITABLE\fP] [\fB\-\-db\-write\-mode\fP \fIMODE\fP]
.sp
\fBocta\fP \fB\-\-mcp\fP
.SH "DESCRIPTION"
.sp
\fBocta\fP is a desktop application for viewing and editing tabular data
files. It opens Parquet, CSV, JSON, SQLite, DuckDB, Excel, and roughly
twenty more formats in a spreadsheet\-like view with sorting, filtering,
full\-text search, inline editing, SQL queries, and file comparison.
.sp
When invoked with no flags, it launches the graphical interface,
optionally opening the supplied \fIFILE\fP(s) in tabs. When invoked with
one of the action flags (\fB\-\-schema\fP, \fB\-\-head\fP, \fB\-\-tail\fP, \fB\-\-sample\fP,
\fB\-\-convert\fP, \fB\-\-sql\fP, \fB\-\-export\-schema\fP, \fB\-\-compare\-schemas\fP, \fB\-\-diff\fP,
\fB\-\-describe\fP, \fB\-\-validate\-schema\fP, \fB\-\-schema\-drift\fP, \fB\-\-harmonise\-schema\fP,
\fB\-\-drift\-report\fP, \fB\-\-check\fP, \fB\-\-relationships\fP,
\fB\-\-report\fP, \fB\-\-fuzzy\-join\fP,
\fB\-\-unique\-columns\fP,
\fB\-\-anonymize\fP,
\fB\-\-dedupe\fP, \fB\-\-impute\fP, \fB\-\-outliers\fP, \fB\-\-detect\-pii\fP, \fB\-\-union\fP,
\fB\-\-join\fP, \fB\-\-partition\-by\fP, \fB\-\-batch\-convert\fP, \fB\-\-resample\fP,
\fB\-\-rolling\fP, \fB\-\-mcp\fP), it performs that action and exits.
.sp
Action flags are mutually exclusive. Trailing \fIFILE\fP arguments are
ignored (with a warning) when an action flag is set.
.sp
A \fIFILE\fP argument may also be a cloud object URL (\f(CRs3://bucket/key\fP,
\f(CRaz://container/blob\fP, \f(CRgs://bucket/key\fP). The object is downloaded to a
temporary file and read as usual. Credentials come from a saved cloud
connection covering the URL, otherwise from the ambient chain (\f(CRAWS_*\fP
environment variables, a cached SSO session, \f(CRaz login\fP, Google application
default credentials). Cloud objects are read\-only here: output still goes to
a local path.
.SH "ACTION FLAGS"
.sp
\fB\-\-schema\fP \fIFILE\fP
.RS 4
Print the column schema of \fIFILE\fP as a two\-column table (column
name, data type). Cheap for streaming formats, since only the header
is read.
.RE
.sp
\fB\-\-head\fP \fIFILE\fP
.RS 4
Print the first \fIN\fP rows of \fIFILE\fP to standard output. \fIN\fP defaults
to 20 and is set with \fB\-n\fP / \fB\-\-lines\fP. For streaming formats, the
reader stops early so this is fast on large files.
.RE
.sp
\fB\-\-tail\fP \fIFILE\fP
.RS 4
Print the last \fIN\fP rows of \fIFILE\fP. \fIN\fP defaults to 20 and is set with
\fB\-n\fP / \fB\-\-lines\fP. Streaming formats load with the standard row cap, so
the tail reflects the end of the loaded window; raise \fB\-\-rows\fP to tail
the true end of a very large file.
.RE
.sp
\fB\-\-sample\fP \fIFILE\fP
.RS 4
Print a random \fIN\fP\-row sample of \fIFILE\fP (without replacement,
original row order preserved). \fIN\fP defaults to 20 (\fB\-n\fP / \fB\-\-lines\fP).
The sample is reproducible for a given \fB\-\-seed\fP (default \fB0\fP).
.RE
.sp
\fB\-\-convert\fP \fIIN\fP \fIOUT\fP
.RS 4
Convert \fIIN\fP to \fIOUT\fP. Both formats are inferred from each path\(cqs
extension and routed through the shared format registry. Read\-only
output formats (SAS, R datasets, HDF5, NetCDF, EPUB, GeoJSON) are
rejected with a clear error.
.RE
.sp
\fB\-\-sql\fP \fIFILE\fP
.RS 4
Run a SQL query against \fIFILE\fP. The query is supplied via
\fB\-q\fP / \fB\-\-query\fP. \fIFILE\fP is exposed to DuckDB as a temporary table
called \fBdata\fP. Extra tables can be registered with \fB\-\-sql\-table\fP
\fINAME\fP=\fIPATH\fP and whole DuckDB / SQLite databases attached with
\fB\-\-sql\-attach\fP \fIALIAS\fP=\fIPATH\fP, so a single invocation can JOIN
across formats. The SELECT result can be persisted to a DuckDB or
SQLite file via \fB\-\-sql\-write\-to\fP. Mutations (INSERT / UPDATE /
DELETE) on \fBdata\fP itself do \fBnot\fP persist back to \fIFILE\fP, since the
in\-memory DuckDB connection is discarded at exit. Use \fB\-\-convert\fP
on the post\-mutation output if you need to persist.
.RE
.sp
\fB\-\-export\-schema\fP \fIFILE\fP
.RS 4
Render \fIFILE\fP\*(Aqs column schema as SQL DDL, a Pydantic model, a
TypeScript interface, a JSON Schema document, or a Rust struct, and
print it to standard output. The target is chosen with \fB\-t\fP /
\fB\-\-target\fP (default \fBpostgres\fP). Only the column list is read and no
rows are serialised.
.RE
.sp
\fB\-\-compare\-schemas\fP \fIFILE_A\fP \fIFILE_B\fP
.RS 4
Diff the column schemas of two files. Prints a four\-column table
(\fBstatus\fP / \fBcolumn\fP / \fBtype_a\fP / \fBtype_b\fP). \fBstatus\fP is one of
\fBcommon\fP, \fBonly_in_a\fP, \fBonly_in_b\fP, \fBtype_mismatch\fP. Matching is by
exact, case\-sensitive column name. For multi\-table sources, the
table to read from each side is selected via \fB\-\-table\-a\fP /
\fB\-\-table\-b\fP.
.RE
.sp
\fB\-\-diff\fP \fIFILE_A\fP \fIFILE_B\fP
.RS 4
Compare two files. \fB\-\-diff\-mode\fP selects the strategy; the output is a
table tagged by a leading \fBstatus\fP column, and a summary line
(per\-mode counts) is written to standard error. Complements
\fB\-\-compare\-schemas\fP, which diffs only the column metadata.
.RE
.sp
\fB\-\-diff\-mode\fP \fIMODE\fP
.RS 4
Comparison strategy for \fB\-\-diff\fP (default \fBset\fP):
\fBset\fP compares rows by whole\-row content (every column, positionally)
and prints rows unique to each side (\fBonly_in_a\fP / \fBonly_in_b\fP); the
files should share the same column order.
\fBordered\fP lines up row \fIi\fP of A with row \fIi\fP of B, prints matched rows
that differ as \fBchanged\fP (with a \fBchanged_columns\fP column) plus any
trailing \fBonly_in_a\fP / \fBonly_in_b\fP rows.
\fBjoin\fP matches rows on the \fB\-\-diff\-on\fP key column(s) and prints
added (\fBonly_in_b\fP), removed (\fBonly_in_a\fP), and \fBchanged\fP rows.
.RE
.sp
\fB\-\-diff\-on\fP \fICOLS\fP
.RS 4
Key column(s) for \fB\-\-diff\-mode join\fP, comma\-separated (matched by
name). Required when the mode is \fBjoin\fP; ignored otherwise.
.RE
.sp
\fB\-\-diff\-db\fP \fICONN\fP
.RS 4
Compare against a live database table instead of a second file.
\fICONN\fP names a saved database connection. With this set, \fB\-\-diff\fP
takes a single positional file: that file is side A and the table is
side B, so \fBonly_in_a\fP means "in the file, not the table". Requires
\fB\-\-diff\-db\-table\fP. The table is read under the usual row cap.
.RE
.sp
\fB\-\-diff\-db\-table\fP \fITABLE\fP
.RS 4
Table to compare against on the \fB\-\-diff\-db\fP connection, as
\fISCHEMA.TABLE\fP or \fICATALOG.SCHEMA.TABLE\fP (the three\-part form is for
Snowflake, Databricks and BigQuery). An unqualified name uses the
connection\(cqs own database.
.RE
.sp
\fB\-\-describe\fP \fIFILE\fP
.RS 4
Print a one\-shot orientation snapshot of \fIFILE\fP: format, file
size, row count, column schema, and a small sample of rows. Use
\fB\-\-sample\-rows\fP to change the preview size (default \fB5\fP, max
\fB100\fP). The \fB\-\-table\fP flag picks a specific table on multi\-table
sources.
.RE
.sp
\fB\-\-validate\-schema\fP \fIFILE\fP
.RS 4
Check \fIFILE\fP\*(Aqs column schema against the JSON Schema given by
\fB\-\-expect\-schema\fP. Exit code is \fB0\fP on a clean match, \fB1\fP
otherwise, which is CI\-pipeable. Schemas produced by \fB\-\-export\-schema \-t
json\-schema\fP round\-trip through this command.
.RE
.sp
\fB\-\-schema\-drift\fP \fIDIR\fP
.RS 4
Scan \fIDIR\fP and report which files disagree about their columns.
Files are grouped by schema, largest group first, so the answer to
a folder of 500 parts is "497 look like this, 3 look like that".
The report table goes to stdout; the per\-variant file lists, any
unreadable files and the drifting column names go to stderr, so a
pipe stays parseable. Exit code is \fB0\fP when every file agrees and
\fB1\fP otherwise, which is CI\-pipeable. Add \fB\-\-recursive\fP to walk
subdirectories (depth 8) and \fB\-\-ignore\-case\fP to treat column names
differing only in case as the same column.
.RE
.sp
\fB\-\-relationships\fP \fIDIR\fP
.RS 4
Rank the likely relationships between the tables in \fIDIR\fP and print
them best first: which column of which table lines up with which
column of which other table. Names take no part in the ranking; the
score comes from how much the values overlap, weighted by how
distinct each side is, so a status column cannot outrank a real key.
Each row also carries \fBorphans\fP, the number of distinct values on
the left with no partner on the right, which is what separates two
candidates that overlap identically. Reads values, so it is capped
at 30 files and at 10,000 rows per table. Unreadable files are noted
on stderr. Always exits \fB0\fP: this is a report, not a gate. Add
\fB\-\-recursive\fP to walk subdirectories.
.RE
.sp
\fB\-\-check\fP \fIFILE\fP
.RS 4
Check \fIFILE\fP\*(Aqs values against the rules in \fB\-\-rules\fP (required) and
report which rules failed. The rules file is TOML, one \f(CR\fP
table per check, naming its column and one of the kinds \fBnot_null\fP,
\fBunique\fP, \fBrange\fP (with \fBmin\fP and \fBmax\fP), \fBregex\fP (with \fBpattern\fP)
or \fBmax_length\fP (with \fBmax_length\fP). A rule without a \f(CRcolumn\fP
applies to every column. The report goes to stdout with one row per
failing rule, its failure count and up to three offending values;
the summary line goes to stderr. Exit code is \fB1\fP on any violation
\fBand\fP on any rule that could not run, because a rules file whose
columns have since been renamed would otherwise report a clean run
over checks it silently skipped. The same rules file is written and
read by the \fBData \(-> Data validation.\|.\|.\fP dialog.
.RE
.sp
\fB\-\-stream\fP
.RS 4
Let DuckDB scan the file where it lies instead of loading its rows.
Applies to \fB\-\-sql\fP over a Parquet, CSV or JSON file, where it lets an
aggregate cover every row of a file far larger than memory: the
primary file is registered as a view named \f(CRdata\fP rather than as a
loaded table, so \fB\-\-rows\fP no longer bounds what the query sees. Every
other action needs the rows themselves and says so on stderr rather
than ignoring the flag. A format DuckDB cannot scan also falls back
to a normal read with a note.
.RE
.sp
\fB\-\-rules\fP \fIFILE\fP
.RS 4
The TOML rules file for \fB\-\-check\fP. Example:
.sp
.if n .RS 4
.nf
.fam C
[[rule]]
column = "order_id"
kind = "unique"

[[rule]]
column = "amount"
kind = "range"
min = 0
.fam
.fi
.if n .RE
.RE
.sp
\fB\-\-drift\-report\fP \fIFILE_A\fP \fIFILE_B\fP
.RS 4
Compare two versions of the same dataset and report how it moved.
Columns are matched by name: one present on a single side is
reported as added or removed, and every shared column gets its null
rate, distinct count and, when numeric, its minimum, maximum and
mean compared. Columns with few enough distinct values also list the
category values that appeared and vanished. Every figure comes from
the same Summary pass the \fBAnalyse \(-> Summary\fP tab uses, so the two
can never disagree. The report goes to stdout; the row counts,
added and removed column names and the pass/fail summary go to
stderr, so a pipe stays parseable. Exit code is \fB0\fP unless
\fB\-\-fail\-on\fP was given and a gate was breached.
.RE
.sp
\fB\-\-fail\-on\fP \fISPEC\fP
.RS 4
Turn \fB\-\-drift\-report\fP into a CI gate. \fISPEC\fP is a comma\-separated
list of \fImetric\fP:_change_ pairs, for example
\f(CRnull_rate:0.05,rows:0.1\fP, where \fIchange\fP is the largest relative
move that still passes. A metric that never appears in the report is
simply never applied. A baseline of zero that moved at all counts as
an unbounded change, so a null rate going from \fB0\fP to \fB0.5\fP breaches
any gate. Metric names are \fBrows\fP, \fBnull_rate\fP, \fBdistinct_count\fP,
\fBmin\fP, \fBmax\fP and \fBmean\fP.
.RE
.sp
\fB\-\-harmonise\-schema\fP \fIDIR\fP
.RS 4
Rewrite every file in \fIDIR\fP to one common set of columns, writing
harmonised copies into \fB\-\-out\-dir\fP (required). The originals are
never modified. The target schema is the shape most files already
have, or the schema of \fB\-\-target\-file\fP when given. A column missing
from a file is added as nulls; a column not in the target is dropped
and named in the report. A file whose values cannot be cast to a
target type is \fBrefused\fP rather than written with nulls in place of
those values, because a harmonised folder of silently emptied cells
looks clean and is not. Two inputs that would write the same output
name are both refused rather than one being renamed. The report goes
to stdout; refusals and dropped columns go to stderr. Exit code is
\fB0\fP when nothing was refused and \fB1\fP otherwise. Add \fB\-\-recursive\fP,
\fB\-\-ignore\-case\fP, and \fB\-\-overwrite\fP to replace existing files in the
output folder.
.RE
.sp
\fB\-\-report\fP \fIOUT.html\fP \fIFILE\fP
.RS 4
Write an HTML profiling report for \fIFILE\fP to \fIOUT.html\fP: per\-column
statistics, distribution charts, the most common values and a
correlation matrix. The document is self\-contained (inline CSS,
inline SVG, no JavaScript) and fetches nothing, so it can be mailed
or published as\-is. \fB\-\-report\-sections\fP \fILIST\fP picks a
comma\-separated subset of \fBstats\fP, \fBdistributions\fP, \fBtop_values\fP and
\fBcorrelation\fP (default: all four); an unknown name is an error rather
than a silent omission. \fB\-\-report\-sample\fP \fIN\fP profiles a random
sample of \fIN\fP rows instead of every row, and the report states that
it sampled.
.RE
.sp
\fB\-\-unique\-columns\fP \fIFILE\fP
.RS 4
Find columns (and optional small combinations) whose values are
unique across \fIFILE\fP. Useful for primary\-key reconnaissance on
undocumented data. Use \fB\-\-max\-combo\fP \fIN\fP (clamped to \fB[1, 3]\fP) to
also test column pairs / triples.
.RE
.sp
\fB\-\-anonymize\fP \fISPEC\fP \fIFILE\fP
.RS 4
Mask / scramble sensitive columns of \fIFILE\fP per the JSON \fISPEC\fP file
and print the sanitised table to stdout. The input file is never
modified. The spec lists per\-column rules (\fBhash\fP / \fBpartial_mask\fP /
\fBredact\fP / \fBfake\fP) plus an optional shared \fBsalt\fP; columns are named.
The same value plus the same salt always maps the same way, so
duplicates stay linked and a re\-run re\-joins to an earlier export.
.RE
.sp
\fB\-\-dedupe\fP \fIFILE\fP
.RS 4
Remove duplicate rows from \fIFILE\fP and print the result to stdout.
Without \fB\-\-dedupe\-on\fP the whole row is the key; with it only the named
columns form the key. \fB\-\-dedupe\-keep\fP (\fBfirst\fP default / \fBlast\fP) picks
the surviving occurrence.
.RE
.sp
\fB\-\-impute\fP \fICOL\fP=\fISTRATEGY\fP
.RS 4
Fill missing/empty cells of a column of the positional \fIFILE\fP.
Repeatable. Strategies: \fBmean\fP, \fBmedian\fP, \fBmode\fP, \fBffill\fP, \fBbfill\fP,
\fBconst:VALUE\fP.
.RE
.sp
\fB\-\-outliers\fP \fIFILE\fP
.RS 4
Flag numeric outlier cells in \fIFILE\fP and print their coordinates.
\fB\-\-outlier\-method\fP (\fBiqr\fP default / \fBzscore\fP), \fB\-\-outlier\-cols\fP
\fICOLS\fP, and \fB\-\-outlier\-k\fP \fIK\fP tune the scan.
.RE
.sp
\fB\-\-detect\-pii\fP \fIFILE\fP
.RS 4
Scan \fIFILE\fP for likely PII columns (email, phone, IBAN, credit card,
SSN) and print the findings. \fB\-\-pii\-sample\fP \fIN\fP sets the per\-column
sample size (default \fB500\fP).
.RE
.sp
\fB\-\-union\fP \fIFILE\fP
.RS 4
Stack the positional \fIFILE\fP plus every \fB\-\-union\-file\fP \fIFILE\fP into one
table (at least two files). \fB\-\-union\-drop\fP \fICOL\fP omits columns and
\fB\-\-union\-cast\fP \fICOL\fP=\fITYPE\fP overrides a column\(cqs target Arrow type.
\fB\-\-union\-ignore\-case\fP treats column names differing only in case as
one column; off by default, and the first spelling encountered names
the output column.
.RE
.sp
\fB\-\-fuzzy\-join\fP \fIFILE\fP
.RS 4
Join the positional \fIFILE\fP to each \fB\-\-fuzzy\-join\-file\fP on how \fBsimilar\fP
the values are rather than on exact equality, for tables that name the
same thing differently (\f(CRMueller GmbH\fP against \f(CRMueller Gmbh.\fP).
\fB\-\-fuzzy\-on\fP \fILEFT\fP=\fIRIGHT\fP names the columns to compare and may be
repeated, in which case the scores are averaged. \fB\-\-fuzzy\-method\fP is
\fBedit_ratio\fP (default), \fBjaro_winkler\fP or \fBtoken_set\fP;
\fB\-\-fuzzy\-threshold\fP is the minimum average score in \fB0.0..=1.0\fP
(default \fB0.85\fP); \fB\-\-fuzzy\-block\fP \fILEFT\fP=\fIRIGHT\fP compares only rows
that agree exactly on those columns, which is what makes a large join
feasible; \fB\-\-fuzzy\-join\-type\fP is \fBinner\fP, \fBleft\fP (default), \fBright\fP or
\fBfull\fP; \fB\-\-fuzzy\-max\-rows\fP caps the rows considered per side (default
\fB20000\fP).
.sp
Each left row keeps its single best partner. The output gains
\fBmatch_score_N\fP and \fBambiguous_N\fP per step; \fBambiguous\fP means the
runner\-up scored nearly as well, so that match is the one to check. The
per\-step report goes to stderr.
.RE
.sp
\fB\-\-join\fP \fIFILE\fP
.RS 4
Join the positional \fIFILE\fP(s) plus every \fB\-\-join\-file\fP \fIFILE\fP on the
\fB\-\-join\-on\fP \fICOLS\fP key(s). \fB\-\-join\-type\fP is \fBleft\fP (default), \fBinner\fP,
\fBright\fP, or \fBfull\fP.
.RE
.sp
\fB\-\-partition\-by\fP \fICOL\fP
.RS 4
Split the positional \fIFILE\fP into one file per distinct value of \fICOL\fP,
written into \fB\-\-out\-dir\fP \fIDIR\fP (required). \fB\-\-partition\-format\fP \fIEXT\fP
sets the output extension (defaults to the source\(cqs).
.RE
.sp
\fB\-\-batch\-convert\fP
.RS 4
Convert every positional \fIFILE\fP into \fB\-\-out\-dir\fP \fIDIR\fP (required) as
\fB\-\-to\fP \fIEXT\fP (required). Output names are \f(CR<stem>.<ext>\fP; two inputs
sharing a stem get \f(CR_2\fP, \f(CR_3\fP suffixes in input order, so a run cannot
overwrite its own earlier output. Existing outputs are skipped unless
\fB\-\-overwrite\fP is given. One failed file does not stop the run.
.sp
Output is a headerless \f(CRinput<TAB>output<TAB>status\fP listing on stdout,
with per\-file errors and a summary on stderr. The exit code is \fB1\fP when
any file failed.
.RE
.sp
\fB\-\-resample\fP \fICOL\fP
.RS 4
Group the positional \fIFILE\fP into time buckets: one row per \fB\-\-interval\fP
of \fICOL\fP. \fB\-\-interval\fP is \fBminute\fP, \fBhour\fP, \fBday\fP (default), \fBweek\fP,
\fBmonth\fP, \fBquarter\fP or \fByear\fP; \fB\-\-value\-cols\fP \fICOLS\fP (required) names the
columns to aggregate; \fB\-\-agg\fP is \fBsum\fP (default), \fBmean\fP, \fBmin\fP, \fBmax\fP,
\fBcount\fP, \fBfirst\fP or \fBlast\fP; \fB\-\-group\-by\fP \fICOLS\fP adds extra grouping
columns, giving one series per combination.
.sp
The time column is cast with \f(CRTRY_CAST\fP, so a row that will not parse
buckets as NULL instead of failing the whole run.
.RE
.sp
\fB\-\-rolling\fP \fICOL\fP
.RS 4
Add a rolling aggregate of \fICOL\fP over the previous \fB\-\-window\fP \fIN\fP rows
(including the current row) to the positional \fIFILE\fP. \fB\-\-order\-by\fP \fICOL\fP
is required \- a rolling aggregate over unordered rows is meaningless.
\fB\-\-agg\fP is \fBmean\fP (default), \fBsum\fP, \fBmin\fP, \fBmax\fP, \fBcount\fP, \fBfirst\fP or
\fBlast\fP; \fB\-\-partition\-by\-cols\fP \fICOLS\fP restarts the frame per group
(spelled that way because \fB\-\-partition\-by\fP is the split\-into\-files
action).
.RE
.sp
\fB\-\-db\-tables\fP
.RS 4
List every schema and table of the saved database connection named by
\fB\-\-db\fP \fICONNECTION\fP (Settings \(-> Databases) as a two\-column table. On
Snowflake, Databricks and BigQuery, which have a catalog level above
the schema, the catalogs themselves are listed unless \fB\-\-db\-catalog\fP
names one.
.RE
.sp
\fB\-\-db\-query\fP \fISQL\fP
.RS 4
Run one SQL statement on the saved connection named by \fB\-\-db\fP,
server\-side and in the engine\(cqs native dialect. SELECT results print in \fB\-f\fP \fIFORMAT\fP;
mutations report rows affected and are refused unless the
connection\(cqs "Allow writes" switch is on. Results stop at the
initial\-load row cap; use \fB\-\-rows\fP to raise or lift it.
.RE
.sp
\fB\-\-sync\-sql\fP \fIFILE\fP
.RS 4
Print the SQL that would make a live database table match \fIFILE\fP, and
write nothing. Reads the table named by \fB\-\-sync\-table\fP through the
connection named by \fB\-\-db\fP, matches rows on \fB\-\-sync\-on\fP, and prints one
transaction to stdout: DELETEs for rows only on the server, full\-row
UPDATEs for rows whose values differ, INSERTs for rows only in the file.
Counts go to stderr, so the script itself pipes cleanly to a file or to
\fBpsql\fP. For teams whose database changes must be reviewed as a script
rather than applied by a tool.
.sp
Numbers are compared as numbers, so a file\(cqs \f(CR120.50\fP and a \f(CRnumeric(12,2)\fP
column\(cqs \f(CR120.50\fP are not a change. Columns present only in the file are
reported on stderr and skipped: this never emits \fBALTER TABLE\fP. Only the
rows loaded under the initial\-load cap are compared; use \fB\-\-rows\fP to raise it.
.RE
.sp
\fB\-\-to\-workbook\fP \fIOUT.xlsx\fP
.RS 4
Write the positional _FILE_s into one Excel workbook, one worksheet per
file. Sheet names come from the file stems and are corrected to Excel\(cqs
rules automatically: at most 31 characters, no forbidden punctuation, and
duplicates numbered \f(CR_2\fP, \f(CR_3\fP. Needs at least two inputs; for a single
file use \fB\-\-convert\fP. Prints a headerless \f(CRinput<TAB>sheet<TAB>rows\fP
listing. Every input is read before anything is written, so an unreadable
file fails the run instead of leaving a half\-written workbook.
.RE
.sp
\fB\-\-sync\-table\fP \fISCHEMA.TABLE\fP
.RS 4
Target table for \fB\-\-sync\-sql\fP. A three\-part \fICATALOG.SCHEMA.TABLE\fP is
accepted for the engines that have catalogs.
.RE
.sp
\fB\-\-sync\-on\fP \fICOLS\fP
.RS 4
Comma\-separated key columns matching file rows to table rows. Required
with \fB\-\-sync\-sql\fP: without a key there is no way to tell an updated row
from a delete plus an insert.
.RE
.sp
\fB\-\-db\-write\-table\fP \fISCHEMA\fP.\fITABLE\fP
.RS 4
Write the positional \fIFILE\fP into the given server table on the \fB\-\-db\fP
connection. \fB\-\-db\-write\-mode\fP is \fBcreate\fP (default; error if the
table exists), \fBappend\fP, or \fBreplace\fP. Refused unless the connection
allows writes.
.RE
.sp
\fB\-\-db\-catalog\fP \fINAME\fP
.RS 4
Catalog (the top namespace level) for a three\-level engine, used by
\fB\-\-db\-tables\fP and \fB\-\-db\-write\-table\fP. Only Snowflake, Databricks and
BigQuery have one; passing it to any other engine is an error. With
\fB\-\-db\-tables\fP and no catalog, the catalogs themselves are listed.
.RE
.sp
\fB\-\-db\-copy\fP \fISCHEMA\fP.\fITABLE\fP
.RS 4
Copy a table from the \fB\-\-db\fP connection to another saved connection,
server to server. Requires \fB\-\-db\-copy\-to\fP. \fB\-\-db\-write\-mode\fP selects
\fBcreate\fP (default), \fBappend\fP or \fBreplace\fP. Refused unless the target
connection allows writes.
.RE
.sp
\fB\-\-db\-copy\-to\fP \fICONNECTION\fP
.RS 4
Target connection for \fB\-\-db\-copy\fP, a saved connection name or id.
.RE
.sp
\fB\-\-db\-copy\-target\fP \fISCHEMA\fP.\fITABLE\fP
.RS 4
Target table for \fB\-\-db\-copy\fP. Defaults to the source schema and table.
.RE
.sp
\fB\-\-db\-copy\-target\-catalog\fP \fINAME\fP
.RS 4
Target catalog for \fB\-\-db\-copy\fP on a three\-level engine.
.RE
.sp
\fB\-\-db\fP \fICONNECTION\fP
.RS 4
Saved connection name (case\-insensitive) or id for the \fB\-\-db\-\fP* actions.
Connections are managed in Settings \(-> Databases; passwords stay in
the system keyring.
.RE
.sp
\fB\-\-mcp\fP
.RS 4
Start a Model Context Protocol (MCP) server on standard input /
output. The tools exposed are: \fBread_table\fP, \fBtail\fP, \fBsample\fP,
\fBschema\fP, \fBlist_tables\fP, \fBcount_rows\fP, \fBrun_sql\fP, \fBconvert\fP,
\fBexport_schema\fP, \fBprofile\fP, \fBfind_duplicates\fP, \fBfuzzy_duplicates\fP,
\fBvalue_frequency\fP, \fBsearch\fP, \fBcompare_schemas\fP, \fBdiff_tables\fP,
\fBdescribe_file\fP, \fBvalidate_against_schema\fP, \fBunique_columns\fP,
\fBpivot\fP, \fBcorrelation\fP, \fBgrep_files\fP, \fBwrite_table\fP, \fBedit_table\fP,
\fBtransform_columns\fP, \fBanonymize\fP, \fBdetect_pii\fP, \fBdetect_outliers\fP,
\fBfill_missing\fP, \fBdrop_duplicates\fP, \fBunion_tables\fP, \fBjoin_tables\fP,
\fBpartition_table\fP, \fBlist_db_connections\fP, \fBlist_db_tables\fP, \fBquery_db\fP,
\fBwrite_db_table\fP, \fBcopy_db_table\fP. The file\-writing tools (\fBconvert\fP, \fBwrite_table\fP,
\fBedit_table\fP, \fBtransform_columns\fP, \fBanonymize\fP, \fBpartition_table\fP, \fBwrite_db_table\fP,
\fBcopy_db_table\fP)
are dropped when \fB\-\-mcp\-read\-only\fP is given. Defaults for the row
limit and per\-cell byte cap come from the user\(cqs Octa settings
(Settings → MCP).
.RE
.sp
\fB\-\-mcp\-read\-only\fP
.RS 4
Only valid with \fB\-\-mcp\fP. Omits the file\-writing tools (\fBwrite_table\fP,
\fBedit_table\fP, \fBconvert\fP) so the server exposes a read\-only surface.
.RE
.sp
\fB\-\-completions\fP \fISHELL\fP
.RS 4
Print a shell completion script for \fISHELL\fP to standard output and exit.
One of \fBbash\fP, \fBzsh\fP, \fBfish\fP, \fBpowershell\fP, \fBelvish\fP. The script is
generated from Octa\(cqs own argument list, so it completes every flag this
page documents. Wire it into the running shell with
\f(CReval "$(octa \-\-completions zsh)"\fP, or let \fBinstall.sh\fP write the files
under the install prefix.
.RE
.sp
\fB\-\-cloud\-ls\fP \fIURL\fP
.RS 4
List a cloud bucket or prefix (\fBs3://\fP, \fBaz://\fP, \fBgs://\fP). One folder
level by default; add \fB\-\-recursive\fP to flatten everything under the
prefix. Credentials come from a saved connection covering the URL, else
the ambient chain.
.RE
.sp
\fB\-\-cloud\-get\fP \fIURL\fP
.RS 4
Download one cloud object to the file given by \fB\-\-out\fP.
.RE
.sp
\fB\-\-cloud\-put\fP \fIFILE\fP
.RS 4
Upload a local file to the cloud URL given by \fB\-\-to\fP.
.RE
.sp
\fB\-\-cloud\-copy\fP \fIURL\fP
.RS 4
Copy a cloud object, or a whole prefix (source ending in \f(CR/\fP), to the URL
given by \fB\-\-to\fP. Within one bucket the backend copies server\-side; across
buckets, accounts or providers the object is streamed in blocks, so object
size does not drive memory. At most 10,000 objects per run.
.RE
.sp
\fB\-\-cloud\-move\fP \fIURL\fP
.RS 4
Like \fB\-\-cloud\-copy\fP, then delete the source. Object stores have no rename.
The delete runs only after every copy succeeded.
.RE
.sp
\fB\-\-cloud\-delete\fP \fIURL\fP
.RS 4
Delete a cloud object, or a whole prefix with \fB\-\-recursive\fP. Cannot be
undone unless the bucket has versioning enabled.
.RE
.sp
\fB\-\-list\-connections\fP
.RS 4
Print the saved cloud and database connections (names and targets only,
never secrets).
.RE
.sp
\fB\-\-add\-connection\fP \fISPEC\fP
.RS 4
Add or update a saved connection from a \fBkey=value,key=value\fP spec.
\fBkind=\fP and \fBname=\fP are required. A connection with the same name is
replaced wholesale, keeping its id and stored secret, so re\-running a
provisioning script is idempotent; omitted keys revert to defaults.
Unknown keys are an error.
Cloud (\fBkind=s3|azure|gcs\fP): bucket, region, endpoint, prefix, account,
profile, account_level, anonymous, allow_writes, force_path_style,
allow_http.
Database (\fBkind=postgres|mysql|mssql|oracle|redshift|clickhouse|exasol|
trino|athena|snowflake|databricks|bigquery\fP): host, port, database, user, allow_writes.
Only password authentication can be expressed here; other methods need the
Settings dialog.
.RE
.sp
\fB\-\-remove\-connection\fP \fINAME\fP
.RS 4
Remove a saved connection by name or id, and delete its stored secret.
.RE
.sp
\fB\-\-secret\-env\fP \fIVAR\fP
.RS 4
Name of an environment variable holding the secret for \fB\-\-add\-connection\fP,
so it never appears in argv. Database: the password. S3:
\fIACCESS_KEY_ID:SECRET_ACCESS_KEY[:TOKEN]\fP. Azure: the account key or a SAS
token. GCS uses application\-default credentials and takes no secret.
.RE
.SH "OPTIONS"
.sp
\fB\-n\fP \fIN\fP, \fB\-\-lines\fP \fIN\fP
.RS 4
Row count for \fB\-\-head\fP, \fB\-\-tail\fP, and \fB\-\-sample\fP. Default \fB20\fP.
.RE
.sp
\fB\-\-seed\fP \fIN\fP
.RS 4
Seed for \fB\-\-sample\fP, for reproducible output. Default \fB0\fP.
.RE
.sp
\fB\-q\fP \fIQUERY\fP, \fB\-\-query\fP \fIQUERY\fP
.RS 4
SQL query string for \fB\-\-sql\fP. Always reference the file\(cqs data as
the table \fBdata\fP.
.RE
.sp
\fB\-\-sql\-table\fP \fINAME\fP=\fIPATH\fP
.RS 4
For \fB\-\-sql\fP only. Register an extra file as a workspace table
named \fINAME\fP. Any supported format. Repeatable.
.RE
.sp
\fB\-\-sql\-attach\fP \fIALIAS\fP=\fIPATH\fP
.RS 4
For \fB\-\-sql\fP only. ATTACH a DuckDB or SQLite database under
\fIALIAS\fP. Repeatable. After attachment every inner table is
queryable as \fBalias.schema.tbl\fP (DuckDB) or \fBalias.tbl\fP (SQLite
when the DuckDB sqlite extension is bundled, otherwise per\-table
fallback registration under \fBalias__table\fP).
.RE
.sp
\fB\-\-sql\-write\-to\fP \fIPATH\fP
.RS 4
For \fB\-\-sql\fP only. Persist the SELECT result to \fIPATH\fP instead of
printing it. The extension picks DuckDB (\fB.duckdb\fP, \fB.ddb\fP) or
SQLite (everything else). Created if missing. Requires
\fB\-\-sql\-write\-table\fP.
.RE
.sp
\fB\-\-sql\-write\-table\fP \fITABLE\fP
.RS 4
Target table name for \fB\-\-sql\-write\-to\fP.
.RE
.sp
\fB\-\-sql\-write\-schema\fP \fISCHEMA\fP
.RS 4
Target schema for \fB\-\-sql\-write\-to\fP. DuckDB only; defaults to
\fBmain\fP. SQLite has no schemas; pass \fBmain\fP or leave unset.
.RE
.sp
\fB\-\-sql\-write\-mode\fP \fIMODE\fP
.RS 4
\fBcreate\fP (default; errors if the table already exists),
\fBreplace\fP (drop + recreate), or \fBappend\fP (INSERT into the
existing table; column count and order must match).
.RE
.sp
\fB\-t\fP \fITARGET\fP, \fB\-\-target\fP \fITARGET\fP
.RS 4
Output target for \fB\-\-export\-schema\fP. \fITARGET\fP is one of \fBpostgres\fP
(default), \fBmysql\fP, \fBsqlite\fP, \fBdatabricks\fP, \fBsnowflake\fP, \fBpydantic\fP,
\fBtypescript\fP, \fBjson\-schema\fP, or \fBrust\fP.
.RE
.sp
\fB\-\-table\-a\fP \fINAME\fP, \fB\-\-table\-b\fP \fINAME\fP
.RS 4
For \fB\-\-compare\-schemas\fP only: the table to read from each side when
the source is multi\-table (SQLite, DuckDB, GeoPackage).
.RE
.sp
\fB\-\-table\fP \fINAME\fP
.RS 4
For \fB\-\-describe\fP, \fB\-\-validate\-schema\fP, \fB\-\-unique\-columns\fP: the
table to read from \fIFILE\fP when the source is multi\-table.
.RE
.sp
\fB\-\-expect\-schema\fP \fISCHEMA_FILE\fP
.RS 4
Path to the expected JSON Schema for \fB\-\-validate\-schema\fP.
Required by that action.
.RE
.sp
\fB\-\-sample\-rows\fP \fIN\fP
.RS 4
Number of preview rows for \fB\-\-describe\fP (default \fB5\fP, clamped
to \fB100\fP).
.RE
.sp
\fB\-\-deep\fP
.RS 4
With \fB\-\-describe\fP, also report how the file is physically written:
row groups, compression codec, encodings and per\-column statistics,
followed by one row per column per row group. Plain\-language hints
about a poor layout (very small row groups, missing statistics, no
compression) go to standard error, so a piped run stays parseable.
Parquet reports full detail; other formats report their size and
state that they expose no inspectable structure. Place it after the
file: \fB\-\-describe FILE \-\-deep\fP.
.RE
.sp
\fB\-\-max\-combo\fP \fIN\fP
.RS 4
Max combo size for \fB\-\-unique\-columns\fP (default \fB1\fP, clamped to
\fB[1, 3]\fP).
.RE
.sp
\fB\-\-rows\fP \fIN\fP|\fBall\fP
.RS 4
Override the initial\-load row cap for this invocation. Streaming
formats (Parquet, CSV, TSV) honour a process\-wide cap (default
\fB5,000,000\fP rows). Pass a number (commas/underscores allowed) to
raise it or \fBall\fP to disable the cap entirely. Applies to
\fB\-\-schema\fP, \fB\-\-head\fP, \fB\-\-convert\fP, and \fB\-\-sql\fP.
.RE
.sp
\fB\-\-dedupe\-on\fP \fICOLS\fP
.RS 4
Comma\-separated key columns for \fB\-\-dedupe\fP. Absent = whole\-row key.
.RE
.sp
\fB\-\-dedupe\-keep\fP \fIWHICH\fP
.RS 4
Which duplicate to keep for \fB\-\-dedupe\fP: \fBfirst\fP (default) or \fBlast\fP.
.RE
.sp
\fB\-\-outlier\-method\fP \fIM\fP
.RS 4
Method for \fB\-\-outliers\fP: \fBiqr\fP (default) or \fBzscore\fP.
.RE
.sp
\fB\-\-outlier\-cols\fP \fICOLS\fP
.RS 4
Comma\-separated columns to scan with \fB\-\-outliers\fP (default: all).
.RE
.sp
\fB\-\-outlier\-k\fP \fIK\fP
.RS 4
Threshold multiplier for \fB\-\-outliers\fP (default \fB1.5\fP IQR, \fB3.0\fP z\-score).
.RE
.sp
\fB\-\-pii\-sample\fP \fIN\fP
.RS 4
Rows sampled per column for \fB\-\-detect\-pii\fP (default \fB500\fP).
.RE
.sp
\fB\-\-union\-file\fP \fIFILE\fP
.RS 4
Additional source for \fB\-\-union\fP (repeatable). The positional file plus
these form the input list (minimum two).
.RE
.sp
\fB\-\-union\-drop\fP \fICOL\fP
.RS 4
Column to omit from the \fB\-\-union\fP output (repeatable).
.RE
.sp
\fB\-\-union\-cast\fP \fICOL\fP=\fITYPE\fP
.RS 4
Override a column\(cqs target Arrow type in the \fB\-\-union\fP output (repeatable).
.RE
.sp
\fB\-\-join\-file\fP \fIFILE\fP
.RS 4
Additional source for \fB\-\-join\fP (repeatable).
.RE
.sp
\fB\-\-join\-on\fP \fICOLS\fP
.RS 4
Key column(s) for \fB\-\-join\fP; comma\-separated or repeated. Required.
.RE
.sp
\fB\-\-join\-type\fP \fITYPE\fP
.RS 4
Join strategy for \fB\-\-join\fP: \fBleft\fP (default), \fBinner\fP, \fBright\fP, \fBfull\fP.
.RE
.sp
\fB\-\-compression\fP \fICODEC\fP
.RS 4
Compression codec for the written file: \fBuncompressed\fP, \fBsnappy\fP,
\fBzstd\fP, \fBgzip\fP or \fBlz4\fP. Applies to \fB\-\-convert\fP and
\fB\-\-batch\-convert\fP; Parquet targets only, ignored by other formats.
An unknown name is rejected before any file is written. Omitted, the
codec saved in \fBSettings > Files > Write options\fP is used, or \fBzstd\fP
when nothing is saved.
.RE
.sp
\fB\-\-row\-group\-size\fP \fIN\fP
.RS 4
Rows per Parquet row group for \fB\-\-convert\fP and \fB\-\-batch\-convert\fP.
Larger groups scan faster; smaller groups let readers skip more
precisely. Omitted means the writer\(cqs own default.
.RE
.sp
\fB\-\-out\-dir\fP \fIDIR\fP
.RS 4
Output directory for \fB\-\-partition\-by\fP (required; created if absent).
.RE
.sp
\fB\-\-partition\-layout\fP \fILAYOUT\fP
.RS 4
How the pieces are named. \f(CRflat\fP (default) writes \f(CR<value>.<ext>\fP side by
side; \f(CRfolder\fP writes \f(CR<value>/part\-0001.<ext>\fP; \f(CRhive\fP writes
\f(CR<column>=<value>/data.<ext>\fP; \f(CRhive\-parts\fP writes
\f(CR<column>=<value>/part\-0001.<ext>\fP. All four hold the same rows and all four
reopen as one table, since the partition column is written into every file.
Only \f(CRflat\fP loses information in the name: it sanitises and lowercases the
value into a file stem, so values differing only in case or punctuation
collide and gain \f(CR_2\fP, \f(CR_3\fP. The folder layouts keep the value as it is,
replacing only characters that cannot appear in a path. \f(CRhive\fP is what
Spark, Athena, DuckDB and pandas expect from a partitioned dataset.
.RE
.sp
\fB\-\-partition\-format\fP \fIEXT\fP
.RS 4
Output extension for \fB\-\-partition\-by\fP (default: the source\(cqs extension).
.RE
.sp
\fB\-f\fP \fIFORMAT\fP, \fB\-\-format\fP \fIFORMAT\fP
.RS 4
Output format for actions that print a table (\fB\-\-schema\fP,
\fB\-\-head\fP, \fB\-\-sql\fP, \fB\-\-compare\-schemas\fP, \fB\-\-describe\fP,
\fB\-\-validate\-schema\fP, \fB\-\-unique\-columns\fP). It has no effect for
\fB\-\-convert\fP (whose output format comes from the output path\(cqs
extension), \fB\-\-export\-schema\fP (which emits source code chosen by
\fB\-t\fP), or \fB\-\-mcp\fP. \fIFORMAT\fP is one of:
.sp
\fBtsv\fP (default)
.RS 4
Tab\-separated values, one row per line, header row first.
.RE
.sp
\fBjson\fP
.RS 4
Pretty\-printed JSON array of \f(CR{column: value}\fP objects. Numeric
and boolean cells keep their native JSON types.
.RE
.sp
\fBcsv\fP
.RS 4
RFC 4180 CSV. Fields with comma, quote, or newline are properly
quoted; embedded quotes are doubled.
.RE
.RE
.sp
\fB\-h\fP, \fB\-\-help\fP
.RS 4
Print the flag list and exit. \fB\-h\fP and \fB\-\-help\fP produce the same
output.
.RE
.sp
\fB\-\-help\-all\fP
.RS 4
Print the flag list plus worked examples for every action, and exit.
The examples are behind their own flag because printing them by
default buried the flag list under something the size of this manual
page.
.RE
.sp
\fB\-\-version\fP
.RS 4
Print the Octa version and exit.
.RE
.SH "OUTPUT STREAMS"
.sp
Tabular data is written to \fBstandard output\fP. Status messages,
warnings, and errors are written to \fBstandard error\fP. This means
\fBocta \-\-sql FILE \-q QUERY \-f json | jq .\|.\|.\fP is safe even when an
error occurs, since the data stream stays clean.
.sp
Exit code is \fB0\fP on success and \fB1\fP on any error (invalid arguments,
file\-not\-found, parse failure, write rejection, etc.).
\fB\-\-validate\-schema\fP also exits \fB1\fP on a successful read where the
schemas differ, and \fB\-\-schema\-drift\fP exits \fB1\fP on a successful scan
where the files disagree. \fB\-\-harmonise\-schema\fP exits \fB1\fP when any file
was refused, \fB\-\-batch\-convert\fP when any input failed,
\fB\-\-drift\-report\fP when a \fB\-\-fail\-on\fP gate was breached, and \fB\-\-check\fP
on any failing or unrunnable rule, so CI pipelines can gate on all six
directly.
.SH "EXAMPLES"
.sp
Open multiple files in the GUI
.RS 4
.sp
.if n .RS 4
.nf
.fam C
octa file1.csv file2.parquet file3.json
.fam
.fi
.if n .RE
.RE
.sp
Print the schema of a Parquet file
.RS 4
.sp
.if n .RS 4
.nf
.fam C
octa \-\-schema sales.parquet
.fam
.fi
.if n .RE
.RE
.sp
Print the first 5 rows of a CSV as JSON
.RS 4
.sp
.if n .RS 4
.nf
.fam C
octa \-\-head data.csv \-n 5 \-f json
.fam
.fi
.if n .RE
.RE
.sp
Print the last rows / a reproducible random sample
.RS 4
.sp
.if n .RS 4
.nf
.fam C
octa \-\-tail data.csv \-n 5
octa \-\-sample data.parquet \-n 20 \-\-seed 1
.fam
.fi
.if n .RE
.RE
.sp
Convert formats
.RS 4
.sp
.if n .RS 4
.nf
.fam C
octa \-\-convert in.csv out.parquet
octa \-\-convert workbook.xlsx tidy.sqlite
.fam
.fi
.if n .RE
.RE
.sp
Group\-by aggregation
.RS 4
.sp
.if n .RS 4
.nf
.fam C
octa \-\-sql sales.parquet \-q \*(AqSELECT region, SUM(amount) FROM data GROUP BY region\*(Aq
.fam
.fi
.if n .RE
.RE
.sp
Read every row of a huge file
.RS 4
.sp
.if n .RS 4
.nf
.fam C
octa \-\-sql huge.parquet \-q \*(AqSELECT count(*) FROM data\*(Aq \-\-rows all
octa \-\-head huge.parquet \-n 100 \-\-rows 10,000,000
.fam
.fi
.if n .RE
.RE
.sp
Pipe a SQL result through \fBjq\fP
.RS 4
.sp
.if n .RS 4
.nf
.fam C
octa \-\-sql users.parquet \-q \*(AqSELECT email FROM data WHERE active\*(Aq \-f json | jq \-r \*(Aq.[].email\*(Aq
.fam
.fi
.if n .RE
.RE
.sp
JOIN across formats
.RS 4
.sp
.if n .RS 4
.nf
.fam C
octa \-\-sql sales.parquet \(rs
     \-\-sql\-table customers=customers.csv \(rs
     \-q \*(AqSELECT c.name, SUM(d.amount) FROM data d
         JOIN customers c ON d.cid = c.cid GROUP BY c.name\*(Aq
.fam
.fi
.if n .RE
.RE
.sp
ATTACH a DuckDB warehouse and JOIN against it
.RS 4
.sp
.if n .RS 4
.nf
.fam C
octa \-\-sql sales.parquet \(rs
     \-\-sql\-attach wh=warehouse.duckdb \(rs
     \-q \*(AqSELECT count(*) FROM data d
         JOIN wh.main.products p ON d.cid = p.cid\*(Aq
.fam
.fi
.if n .RE
.RE
.sp
Write a SQL result back to a DuckDB schema
.RS 4
.sp
.if n .RS 4
.nf
.fam C
octa \-\-sql sales.parquet \-q \*(AqSELECT region, SUM(amount) AS total
                             FROM data GROUP BY region\*(Aq \(rs
     \-\-sql\-write\-to analytics.duckdb \(rs
     \-\-sql\-write\-schema reports \-\-sql\-write\-table q4_summary
.fam
.fi
.if n .RE
.RE
.sp
Export a schema as Snowflake DDL
.RS 4
.sp
.if n .RS 4
.nf
.fam C
octa \-\-export\-schema sales.parquet \-t snowflake
octa \-e users.parquet \-t pydantic > users_model.py
.fam
.fi
.if n .RE
.RE
.sp
Diff two schemas
.RS 4
.sp
.if n .RS 4
.nf
.fam C
octa \-\-compare\-schemas v1.parquet v2.parquet
octa \-\-compare\-schemas a.sqlite b.sqlite \-\-table\-a users \-\-table\-b users \-f json
.fam
.fi
.if n .RE
.RE
.sp
Diff two files\*(Aq rows
.RS 4
.sp
.if n .RS 4
.nf
.fam C
octa \-\-diff v1.csv v2.csv
octa \-\-diff v1.csv v2.csv \-\-diff\-mode ordered
octa \-\-diff v1.csv v2.csv \-\-diff\-mode join \-\-diff\-on id
octa \-\-diff a.parquet b.parquet \-f json
.fam
.fi
.if n .RE
.RE
.sp
One\-shot file snapshot
.RS 4
.sp
.if n .RS 4
.nf
.fam C
octa \-\-describe data.parquet \-\-sample\-rows 3
octa \-\-describe users.sqlite \-\-table customers \-f json
.fam
.fi
.if n .RE
.RE
.sp
Validate a file against a JSON Schema in CI
.RS 4
.sp
.if n .RS 4
.nf
.fam C
octa \-\-export\-schema sales.parquet \-t json\-schema > sales.schema.json
octa \-\-validate\-schema sales.parquet \-\-expect\-schema sales.schema.json
.fam
.fi
.if n .RE
.RE
.sp
Find primary\-key candidates
.RS 4
.sp
.if n .RS 4
.nf
.fam C
octa \-\-unique\-columns users.csv
octa \-\-unique\-columns orders.parquet \-\-max\-combo 2 \-f json
.fam
.fi
.if n .RE
.RE
.sp
Browse a live database connection
.RS 4
.sp
.if n .RS 4
.nf
.fam C
octa \-\-db\-tables \-\-db warehouse
octa \-\-db\-tables \-\-db warehouse \-\-db\-catalog sales_prod
octa \-\-db\-query "SELECT * FROM public.users LIMIT 10" \-\-db prod

# Review the change before it touches the server
octa \-\-sync\-sql users.csv \-\-db prod \-\-sync\-table public.users \-\-sync\-on id > change.sql

# One workbook from several tables
octa \-\-to\-workbook report.xlsx sales.csv returns.parquet stock.json
.fam
.fi
.if n .RE
.RE
.sp
Copy a table between two servers, replacing the target
.RS 4
.sp
.if n .RS 4
.nf
.fam C
octa \-\-db prod \-\-db\-copy analytics.orders \(rs
     \-\-db\-copy\-to warehouse \-\-db\-write\-mode replace
.fam
.fi
.if n .RE
.RE
.sp
Start the MCP server
.RS 4
.sp
.if n .RS 4
.nf
.fam C
octa \-\-mcp
.fam
.fi
.if n .RE
.RE
.SS "Pipelines"
.sp
\f(CR\-\fP stands for standard input as a \fIFILE\fP, and for standard output as the
\f(CR\-\-convert\fP target, so octa fits in the middle of a shell pipeline.
.sp
.if n .RS 4
.nf
.fam C
# Read a table from a pipe: the format is sniffed from the bytes
curl \-s https://example.org/sales.csv | octa \-\-schema \-

# Query it without ever writing a file
cat sales.csv | octa \-\-sql \- \-q "SELECT city, SUM(amount) FROM data GROUP BY city"

# Convert on the way through; \-\-to says what to write, since a pipe has no name
cat sales.csv | octa \-\-convert \- \- \-\-to json | jq \*(Aq.[0]\*(Aq
.fam
.fi
.if n .RE
.sp
Reports and counts go to stderr, so what reaches the next command in the
pipeline is exactly the data. Piped input is buffered to a temporary file
first: every reader needs to seek (a Parquet footer is at the end of the
file), so a pipe cannot be streamed straight through.
.SS "Reading from a URL"
.sp
Anywhere a \fIFILE\fP is accepted, a URL works too.
.sp
.if n .RS 4
.nf
.fam C
# A plain web address: downloaded to a temp file, no configuration needed
octa \-\-schema https://example.org/exports/sales.csv
octa \-\-sql "SELECT * FROM data LIMIT 5" https://example.org/exports/sales.csv

# Cloud object storage resolves through the saved connections instead
octa \-\-schema s3://bucket/sales.parquet
.fam
.fi
.if n .RE
.sp
The format comes from the URL\(cqs path, ignoring any query string, so a signed
link ending \f(CRsales.csv?token=.\|.\|.\fP still reads as CSV; a URL with no extension
falls back to content sniffing. Both forms are read\-only, and a non\-2xx
response is an error naming the status code rather than a file, so a 404 HTML
page is never parsed as a one\-column table.
.sp
Tool\-supplied URLs are restricted. When the address comes from an agent (the
MCP server or the in\-app assistant) rather than from you, Octa resolves the
host first and refuses anything that is not globally routable: loopback,
private ranges, link\-local (which includes the cloud metadata endpoint at
169.254.169.254), and unique\-local IPv6. Redirects are not followed on that
path either, since a redirect is how a check on the first hop gets bypassed.
Addresses you type yourself, on the command line or in File > Open URL, are
not restricted: reading from \f(CRlocalhost:8000\fP is a normal thing to ask for.
.SS "Cloud object storage"
.sp
.if n .RS 4
.nf
.fam C
octa \-\-cloud\-ls s3://bucket/prefix/
octa \-\-cloud\-ls s3://bucket/ \-\-recursive

octa \-\-cloud\-get s3://bucket/data.parquet \-\-out ./data.parquet
octa \-\-cloud\-put ./data.parquet \-\-to s3://bucket/data.parquet

octa \-\-cloud\-copy s3://a/data/ \-\-to gs://b/backup/
octa \-\-cloud\-move s3://a/old.csv \-\-to s3://a/archive/old.csv
octa \-\-cloud\-delete s3://a/old.csv
octa \-\-cloud\-delete s3://a/scratch/ \-\-recursive

octa \-\-list\-connections
.fam
.fi
.if n .RE
.SS "Managing connections headlessly"
.sp
.if n .RS 4
.nf
.fam C
export S3_KEY=\*(AqAKIAEXAMPLE:wJalrXUtnFEMI\*(Aq
octa \-\-add\-connection \*(Aqkind=s3,name=prod,bucket=my\-bucket,region=eu\-central\-1,allow_writes=true\*(Aq \(rs
     \-\-secret\-env S3_KEY

octa \-\-add\-connection \*(Aqkind=postgres,name=warehouse,host=db.internal,database=analytics,user=reader\*(Aq \(rs
     \-\-secret\-env PGPASSWORD

octa \-\-remove\-connection warehouse
.fam
.fi
.if n .RE
.SH "ENVIRONMENT"
.sp
\fBOCTA_CONFIG_DIR\fP
.RS 4
Directory holding \fBsettings.toml\fP, used verbatim on every platform and
taking precedence over \fBXDG_CONFIG_HOME\fP / \fBHOME\fP / \fBAPPDATA\fP. Required in
containers, which usually set none of those; without it Octa reports that
it has no config directory rather than silently running without settings.
.RE
.sp
\fBOCTA_DEBUG\fP
.RS 4
Set to 1 to force debug mode on for one run without changing the saved
setting: verbose logging, plus one log line per mouse press and release
recording its position, whether it counted as a click, and which layer of
the interface it reached. Intended for diagnosing the GUI itself, when the
Settings dialog is the thing misbehaving and its checkbox cannot be reached.
A build from source additionally outlines every clickable area on screen;
release binaries cannot, since the toolkit compiles that drawing out.
.RE
.sp
\fBOCTA_NO_KEYRING\fP
.RS 4
Set to 1 to skip the OS keyring entirely, so secrets are read from and
written to \fBsettings.toml\fP (chmod 0600). Secrets already fall back to the
file when the keyring errors; this avoids the lookup on systems where it
can never work, such as a container with no D\-Bus.
.RE
.sp
\fBAWS_ACCESS_KEY_ID\fP, \fBAWS_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY\fP, \fBAZURE_STORAGE_ACCOUNT\fP, \fBGOOGLE_APPLICATION_CREDENTIALS\fP
.RS 4
Ambient cloud credentials, used when no saved connection covers a URL.
.RE
.SH "FILES"
.sp
\f(CR\fI$XDG_CONFIG_HOME/octa/settings.toml\fP\fP
.RS 4
Linux. User settings. Created on first launch with defaults.
.RE
.sp
\f(CR\fI$HOME/Library/Application Support/Octa/settings.toml\fP\fP
.RS 4
macOS. Same purpose.
.RE
.sp
\f(CR\fI%APPDATA%\(rsOcta\(rssettings.toml\fP\fP
.RS 4
Windows. Same purpose.
.RE
.SH "MCP SERVER"
.sp
When invoked with \fB\-\-mcp\fP, Octa speaks the Model Context Protocol
over JSON\-RPC on stdin/stdout. The tools are:
.sp
.RS 4
.ie n \{\
\h'-04'\(bu\h'+03'\c
.\}
.el \{\
.  sp -1
.  IP \(bu 2.3
.\}
\fBread_table(path, limit?, unlimited?, table?)\fP: schema + rows JSON.
.RE
.sp
.RS 4
.ie n \{\
\h'-04'\(bu\h'+03'\c
.\}
.el \{\
.  sp -1
.  IP \(bu 2.3
.\}
\fBtail(path, n?, unlimited?, table?)\fP: last N rows.
.RE
.sp
.RS 4
.ie n \{\
\h'-04'\(bu\h'+03'\c
.\}
